sections.body:
An `if` statement evaluates a condition's truthiness (see the Data Types concept for what counts as truthy/falsy) and only runs its body when the condition is true. `elif` (short for 'else if') lets you chain additional conditions, and `else` catches everything not matched by any prior branch.

The crucial thing to internalize is that Python checks the conditions **top to bottom and stops at the first one that's true** -- at most one branch in an if/elif/.../else chain ever runs, even if multiple conditions would technically evaluate to true. This is different from writing several independent `if` statements in a row, where every single one is checked and potentially executed regardless of the others:

```python
score = 85

# elif chain: only ONE branch runs
if score >= 90:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 80:
    grade = "B"      # this one runs; score >= 70 is never even checked
elif score >= 70:
    grade = "C"
else:
    grade = "F"

# separate ifs: EVERY condition is checked independently
if score >= 90:
    print("Excellent")
if score >= 80:
    print("Good")     # this prints
if score >= 70:
    print("Passing")  # this ALSO prints, even though grade was already decided as B above
```

Using separate `if` statements when you meant a single elif-chain is a common source of bugs where multiple branches unexpectedly run together.

examples.code:
for i in range(1, 16):
    if i % 15 == 0:
        print("FizzBuzz")
    elif i % 3 == 0:
        print("Fizz")
    elif i % 5 == 0:
        print("Buzz")
    else:
        print(i)

